course: dictionary
lesson: 7417
title: One word, many meanings: pole
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Pole is one of them. The words around it, its context, tell you which meaning is right. Read the whole sentence before you decide.
explain: «A punt is a flat-bottomed boat pushed along with a long pole on shallow rivers.» Here a pole is a long stick. The boat is pushed along with it, so pole must mean a stick, not a point on the Earth.
explain: «The North Pole and the South Pole are the two ends of the Earth's axis.» Here a pole is a point, the most northern or southern place on the Earth. Ask yourself: what would fit in this sentence? A stick does not fit, but a point does.
explain: A capital letter can change a word too. Pole with a capital P means a person from Poland. Try your meaning in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: A pole is a long thin stick, often made of wood or metal, that stands up straight or is held.
fact: «The North Pole» and «the South Pole» are the most northern and southern points of the Earth.
fact: A magnet has two poles, north and south.
fact: A Pole with a capital letter is a person from Poland.
fact: «Poles apart» means completely different.
exercise: «A punt is a flat-bottomed boat pushed along with a long pole on shallow rivers.» What is a pole in this sentence? | A rope to tie a boat up | *A long stick | A person from Poland | A point on the Earth
exercise: «The North Pole and the South Pole are the two ends of the Earth's axis.» What are the poles in this sentence? | Two people from Poland | *Points at the most northern and southern ends of the Earth | Two long sticks for a tent | Two magnets
exercise: A magnet has two poles. Which two are they? | Left and right | Hot and cold | Up and down | *North and south
test: Jade says, «The two of them are poles apart.» What does poles apart mean? | Standing very close together | Lost at sea | Very tall | *Completely different
test: Which meaning of pole is correct for a capital P? | *A person from Poland | A north point on the Earth | A long stick | A magnet's end
exam: «It sends the numbers by invisible radio waves to the nearest phone mast, often a pole on a roof you have walked past a hundred times.» What does pole mean here? | A type of boat | A north point on the Earth | *A tall thin post standing up | A person from Poland
